; What Fedora's SELinux policy lacks for two PAM modules the image runs where Fedora does not expect them,
; installed by build_files/base/08-hardening.sh. tests/vm/suites/83-logins.sh shows each rule at work.

; Parental Controls' allowed hours (pam_malcontent, in login and sshd) ask accountsservice for the account's
; limits over D-Bus. The text console (local_login_t) and OpenSSH's per-connection process (sshd_session_t)
; may not make that call, so the module fails, its line lets the login through, and a child logs in at any
; hour. This is what the policy's accountsd_dbus_chat() gives a domain.
(allow local_login_t accountsd_t (dbus (send_msg)))
(allow accountsd_t local_login_t (dbus (send_msg)))
(allow sshd_session_t accountsd_t (dbus (send_msg)))
(allow accountsd_t sshd_session_t (dbus (send_msg)))

; One-time codes for SSH (pam_google_authenticator) rewrite ~/.google_authenticator after every code, for
; the codes used once and the rate limit, through a new file named .google_authenticator~XXXXXX. The policy
; labels it auth_home_t only under the exact name .google_authenticator~, so it would take the home folder's
; own type, which sshd may not create, and every login with a code would fail. The module's file is the
; only one sshd's per-connection process makes directly in a home folder.
(typetransition sshd_session_t user_home_dir_t file auth_home_t)
